LAYER 6 — NARRATIVE VS DATA DIVERGENCE + STATE TRANSITION
Compare what the quarter actually said vs what the narrative emphasised:
- Is the narrative too bullish relative to the print?
- Is the narrative too negative relative to actual composition?
- Is the market focusing on the wrong metric?
- Is the guide driving the reaction more than the quarter?
◆◆◆ - Which direction does the narrative-data lag run, and at what speed? (US-season baseline: narratives converged to data within hours; EU baseline observed: narratives can lag improving data by a quarter — state the lag direction explicitly.)

Mandatory close ◆◆ (extended per §13-bis ◆◆◆ and two-sided falsification where applicable):
State transition: [label] — [one-sentence rationale] / Falsification: [mechanism-based condition]

Labels: continuation / stabilization / improvement / deterioration / reversal / false dawn / no material change
◆◆◆ Compound-state qualifiers are permitted and encouraged where one label misleads: "improvement — decisive", "deterioration (mild)", "improvement (early-stage, unpaid)", "deterioration (cyclical) with structural-share improvement", "deterioration (operating) within an unbroken narrative state", "improvement (fundamental) with capital-return doctrine break". The base label remains from the controlled list; the qualifier carries the composition. One state line per tape.

This line is mandatory in every tape. It cannot be omitted.

◆◆ State-transition discipline: The label describes the release relative to the prior verified state of the name/thesis — not merely beat/miss. Do not equate: consensus beat with improvement; consensus miss with deterioration; a guide raise with thesis improvement when the composition contradicts it; a stock rally with a stronger fundamental state.

◆◆ False-dawn gating: "False dawn" is a controlled label. Use it at release level only when (a) a standing thematic overlay (§XIV) is active with a defined validation/falsification trigger, and (b) the current print materially bears on that trigger. Absent an active overlay, use reversal / stabilization / improvement instead.

◆ Setup-driven reactions: Layer 6 must distinguish between (a) fundamental signal-driven stock reactions and (b) setup-driven stock reactions. The "beat-and-dip" pattern is the canonical example. Tape must explicitly state whether the reaction is setup-driven, fundamental, or both, and flag the differential for the PM. ◆◆ When §11-bis concurrent drivers also apply, both classifications must appear. ◆◆◆ Apply the §XVI pricing-regime lens to every reaction classification.

