◆◆◆ XVI. REACTION-PRICING REGIME LENS (NEW — derived from the Q2 2026 47-tape production build)

This section codifies the empirically derived pricing regime as an ANALYTICAL LENS for Layer 6 classification. It is a classification aid and hypothesis set, not a prediction engine; the scout applies it to explain reactions and to specify re-rate triggers, and flags prints that violate it (a violated regime rule is itself a Layer 6 finding).

A. THE PRICING FUNCTION (elevated-bar regime, as of the 2026 cohort):
reaction ≈ f( guidance-instrument quality × signed positioning × narrative resolution )
Beat magnitude carries approximately zero weight at elevated setups.

B. INSTRUMENT-QUALITY GRADIENT (hard → soft currency):
1. Contracted dollar backlog / RPO with composition disclosure (hardest)
2. Take-or-pay LTAs / signed supply agreements
3. Forward-loaded guidance raises (decomposed % of raise that is unreported future revenue)
4. Rolling customer forecasts / multi-year commitments (dated)
5. "Up to" GW/PPA/capacity frames (soft)
6. ARR/run-rate headlines without contracted-metric conversion
7. "Midpoint opportunity" ranges excluded from guidance
8. Trust / withheld forward frames (charged at a premium)
Corollaries: backlog admits; income-statement conversion + intact capital returns price; forward opacity is charged; first-party builders with no backlog instrument pay a structural discount until they invent an equivalent instrument. Sub-threshold improvements (stabilization-grade evidence) do not re-rate standing trends — diagnose the instrument gap and state the re-rate trigger.

C. SIGNED POSITIONING TERM:
Positioning enters with a SIGN, not just a size. Short-crowded names detonate on step-changes (squeeze amplification, moves exceeding options-implied); long-crowded names fade identical instrument quality (sold-the-news at any print quality); at extreme setups (doubled-YTD) the positioning term saturates and no instrument clears the bar — the next re-rate window opens on drawdown, not evidence. Negative-setup + genuine step-change = maximum re-rate. Setup context in Layer 0 is therefore mandatory (§I).

D. EXCEPTION CLAUSES (named, apply as tests):
- Narrative-falsification clause: falsifying a standing sector bear narrative can substitute for a guidance step-change (modest beat → outsized re-rate).
- Spend-denomination clause: at a regime boundary, a step-change denominated in SPEND rather than demand-conversion fails even when large.
- Story-stock exemption / two-tier regime: the evidence-demand function is conditional on the valuation anchor — P&L-priced names are audited ruthlessly; narrative-priced names trade on non-breakage of unfalsifiable forward lines and large operating misses can price at near-zero. State which tier the name trades in.
- Accounting-quality discipline runs both directions: the market strips §13 items whether the strip flatters or reveals a miss.

E. USAGE: every Layer 6 reaction classification cites the regime term(s) that explain it; every unpaid improvement states its re-rate trigger; every violation of A–D is flagged as a potential regime change and escalated in the PM note.

◆◆◆ XVI-B. TRANSMISSION-CHAIN TRACKING (NEW):
A transmission chain is a documented causal path across multiple tapes (e.g., crude premium → airline fuel → fare recapture → household demand; memory pricing → hyperscaler capex line → device BOM → retail price → unit-demand watch; hyperscaler backlog → capex → foundry/memory → equipment revenue → power). Chains are named, their links cited to specific tapes, their open (unevidenced) links stated, and their terminal watch items assigned to named future prints. A tape that closes or extends a chain link says so in Layer 1/6. Chains feed the season recap's forward section.

================================================================================

◆◆◆ XVII. STANDING REGISTERS (NEW — cross-tape ledgers, maintained per season)

Registers are cumulative, cited in read-across tables, restated at build close, and carried into the season recap. A tape that triggers a register updates it in its PM note/audit trail. Register entries carry: name, date, magnitude, family/class, and any reversal schedule.

Active register classes (2026 production set — extend as needed):
1. §13 NON-OPERATING REGISTER — by family per §13-ter-A, ranked by magnitude, with reversibility flags (mark-to-market books, post-quarter markdowns tracked as scheduled narrative events).
2. IEEPA / TARIFF-REFUND REGISTER — entry per company with magnitude (or "unquantified — 10-Q patch pending"), usage doctrine (optics / margin flatterer / competitive ammunition), and the two-sided forward warning (flatters current-year comps; reverses as recoveries end / new levies land).
3. AI-LABOR REALLOCATION REGISTER — workforce actions tied to AI adoption/spend, by company and scale.
4. CUSTOM-SILICON REGISTER — in-house silicon programs competing for foundry/packaging capacity, with scale datapoints (run-rates, merchant sales).
5. CIRCULAR-FINANCING NODE MAP — node-level (counterparty-level), not company-level: for each central node (e.g., a frontier lab), map the legs — equity-marked-by, compute-committed-to, credit-extended-by (receivables, vendor financing), equity-funded-by, energy/infrastructure JV exposure. Watch items: receivable aging, markdown flow-through, cross-default concentration. Concentration analysis at the NODE is the deliverable.
6. CONSTRAINT-MIGRATION CHAIN — the current binding physical constraint by layer (packaging → spares → memory → WFE output → construction/gen-sets → megawatts …), updated as prints move the bottleneck.
7. DISCLOSURE-REGIME LEDGER — metric retirements, withheld frames, and newly invented disclosure instruments (backlog per-quarter deltas, conversion metrics), as an information-regime quality track.
8. INSIDER / CONTROLLING-SHAREHOLDER SIGNALS — directional-only where magnitudes unverifiable.

